  "content": {
    "id": "p30-q14-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "14",
    "prompt": "Consider the function\n$$f(x)=\\frac{ax+b}{cx+d},$$\nwhere $a,b,c,d\\in\\mathbb R$ and $c,d$ are not both zero. Take the codomain of $f$ to be its range.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p30-q14-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Determine the implied domain of $f$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\mathbb R\\setminus\\{-\\frac{d}{c}\\}$ if $c\\ne0$; $\\mathbb R$ if $c=0$.",
          "worked": "The denominator must be nonzero: $cx+d\\ne0$.\n\nIf $c\\ne0$, this excludes $x=-\\frac{d}{c}$, so $\\operatorname{dom}(f)=\\mathbb R\\setminus\\{-\\frac{d}{c}\\}$.\n\nIf $c=0$, then $d\\ne0$, so $\\operatorname{dom}(f)=\\mathbb R$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 24
      },
      {
        "id": "p30-q14-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Suppose $f$ is bijective. Find the rule for $f^{-1}$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$f^{-1}(x)=\\dfrac{b-dx}{cx-a}$.\n\nInterchange $x$ and $y$, then collect the terms containing $y$.",
          "worked": "Interchange $x$ and $y$ and solve for $y$:\n$$\\begin{align*}x&=\\frac{ay+b}{cy+d}\\\\cxy+dx&=ay+b\\\\cxy-ay&=b-dx\\\\y(cx-a)&=b-dx\\\\y&=\\frac{b-dx}{cx-a}.\\end{align*}$$\nHence $f^{-1}(x)=\\dfrac{b-dx}{cx-a}$.\n\nFor $c\\ne0$, its domain is $\\mathbb R\\setminus\\{\\frac{a}{c}\\}$ and its range is $\\mathbb R\\setminus\\{-\\frac{d}{c}\\}$. For $c=0$, bijectivity requires $a\\ne0$, and $f^{-1}(x)=\\dfrac{dx-b}{a}$ has domain and range $\\mathbb R$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 48
      },
      {
        "id": "p30-q14-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "Prove that $f$ is bijective if and only if $ad-bc\\ne0$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$f$ is bijective if and only if $ad-bc\\ne0$.\n\nIf $ad-bc=0$, $f$ is constant. Otherwise, $g(x)=\\dfrac{b-dx}{cx-a}$ satisfies both inverse-composition identities.",
          "worked": "If $ad-bc=0$ and $c\\ne0$, then $b=\\frac{ad}{c}$, so\n$$f(x)=\\frac{a(x+d/c)}{c(x+d/c)}=\\frac ac$$\nthroughout its domain. If $c=0$, then $d\\ne0$ and $ad=0$, giving $a=0$ and $f(x)=\\frac{b}{d}$. In either case $f$ is constant on a domain containing distinct points, so it is not injective and therefore not bijective.\n\nNow suppose $\\Delta=ad-bc\\ne0$. If $c\\ne0$, solving $f(x)=y$ gives $x=\\frac{(b-dy)}{(cy-a)}$ for every $y\\ne \\frac{a}{c}$. This input lies in the domain because\n$$c\\frac{b-dy}{cy-a}+d=\\frac{-\\Delta}{cy-a}\\ne0.$$\nAlso, $f(x)$ cannot equal $\\frac{a}{c}$, since that would imply $bc=ad$. Thus the range is $\\mathbb R\\setminus\\{\\frac{a}{c}\\}$. If $c=0$, then $a,d\\ne0$, and the range is $\\mathbb R$.\n\nDefine $g(x)=\\dfrac{b-dx}{cx-a}$ on this range. For each input in the appropriate domain,\n$$\\begin{align*}g(f(x))&=\\frac{b(cx+d)-d(ax+b)}{c(ax+b)-a(cx+d)}\\\\g(f(x))&=\\frac{(bc-ad)x}{bc-ad}\\\\g(f(x))&=x.\\end{align*}$$\nSimilarly,\n$$\\begin{align*}f(g(x))&=\\frac{a(b-dx)+b(cx-a)}{c(b-dx)+d(cx-a)}\\\\f(g(x))&=\\frac{(bc-ad)x}{bc-ad}\\\\f(g(x))&=x.\\end{align*}$$\nBoth identities hold because $bc-ad\\ne0$. Hence $g$ is the inverse of $f$, so $f$ is bijective. Together the two cases prove the result.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 112
      }
    ]
  },
